# shell: desktop
# The thesis, in one window: a board ships NO interface code.  It says
# in a few lines what it has and which PLACE each control shows or
# changes, and the desktop is the program that draws it -- with the
# same controls, the same theme and the same narration as everything
# else here, because it IS everything else here.
wait 12
note the description opens as a panel, titled what it called itself
click-row 3
wait 3
click-row 3
wait 12
expect-title Board one
note the places are read, so the panel shows what IS rather than what
note somebody typed into a mock-up: the name field carries the board's
expect-narrate workshop
note and the gauge reads the temperature the board wrote down
expect-narrate gauge
note a row this build has never heard of is COUNTED and said out loud,
note not silently dropped: a panel from a newer device is short
expect-narrate does not know
note working the switch WRITES to the place the board named -- which is
note the whole of the interface
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_board/led 0
click 480 272
wait 12
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_board/led 1
quit
